WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.390
FlashLib brings the ideas of fast

2
00:00:02.453 --> 00:00:04.607
GPU attention kernels to classical

3
00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.380
machine-learning operators.

4
00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.330
Rewrite the operator so

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.930
that it suits the GPU.

6
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.760
Never write a large table to memory.

7
00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.350
Keep running minima in registers.

8
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.458
And split a squared distance

9
00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:19.520
so that the costly part becomes a

10
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.460
matrix product on tensor cores.

11
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.680
Take brute-force k-nearest neighbors.

12
00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.811
The naive version fills the

13
00:00:26.876 --> 00:00:28.525
whole table of distances

14
00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:29.910
then sorts each row.

15
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.144
For our four queries and four

16
00:00:32.209 --> 00:00:33.833
thousand ninety-six rows

17
00:00:33.898 --> 00:00:36.366
that is sixteen thousand three hundred

18
00:00:36.431 --> 00:00:37.860
eighty-four distances.

19
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.936
A GPU version streams the

20
00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:41.524
rows through in tiles

21
00:00:41.524 --> 00:00:44.285
and keeps only the best five per query

22
00:00:44.285 --> 00:00:46.770
twenty values, and the same answer.

23
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.030
Claude Opus 5.5 passed with a pruning idea.

24
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.202
Its first pass keeps only the two smallest

25
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.420
distances in each group of rows.

26
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.957
The k smallest group minima are k real rows

27
00:01:00.029 --> 00:01:01.912
so the k-th of them bounds

28
00:01:01.984 --> 00:01:03.650
the true k-th distance.

29
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.960
A group below the bound gives its best row.

30
00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.843
A group whose second value is also below

31
00:01:09.907 --> 00:01:11.870
the bound is rescanned in full.

32
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.180
Every other row is provably too far.

33
00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.227
In our model, with groups of thirty-two

34
00:01:18.296 --> 00:01:19.679
and k equal to five

35
00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.961
only five rows are scored again

36
00:01:21.961 --> 00:01:23.690
and the answer is exact.

37
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.593
Pause and predict.

38
00:01:25.593 --> 00:01:27.652
Why rescan the whole group

39
00:01:27.652 --> 00:01:29.710
not just its two kept rows?

40
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.431
Because only two values were kept.

41
00:01:35.431 --> 00:01:37.602
If the second is below the bound

42
00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:39.199
later rows of that group

43
00:01:39.263 --> 00:01:40.540
may be below it too.

44
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.660
All four FlashLib tasks are judged alike.

45
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.179
The agent writes a GPU version

46
00:01:47.252 --> 00:01:48.770
in Python and Triton.

47
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.059
The score is the latency score

48
00:01:51.059 --> 00:01:52.560
over the plain starter.

49
00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.601
A pass must be strictly faster than the

50
00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.017
human reference on every workload

51
00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.708
with quality no worse, or for k-means

52
00:02:00.708 --> 00:02:01.950
within set bands.

53
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.420
On k-NN, two of sixteen passed.

54
00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.518
Gemini 4 Argon in Pi was

55
00:02:07.591 --> 00:02:09.193
faster on average too

56
00:02:09.266 --> 00:02:11.815
but its worst exact recall was zero

57
00:02:11.888 --> 00:02:14.438
point nine nine nine nine, not one

58
00:02:14.438 --> 00:02:15.530
and it failed.

59
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.322
One k-means step sends every point

60
00:02:18.387 --> 00:02:19.818
to its nearest center

61
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:21.509
then moves each center to

62
00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:23.070
the mean of its points.

63
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.700
A GPU version computes the distances

64
00:02:25.763 --> 00:02:27.853
to one tile of centers at a time

65
00:02:27.853 --> 00:02:29.183
as a matrix product

66
00:02:29.183 --> 00:02:31.020
and keeps a running minimum.

67
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.529
Opus's own trick was to write the tile's number

68
00:02:34.592 --> 00:02:36.960
into the lowest bits of each distance.

69
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.746
Then a plain minimum carries the winning

70
00:02:39.807 --> 00:02:41.390
tile along with the value.

71
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.648
In our model, with three bits

72
00:02:43.712 --> 00:02:45.302
the tile is right for all

73
00:02:45.366 --> 00:02:46.574
six hundred points

74
00:02:46.638 --> 00:02:48.292
and the error is under one

75
00:02:48.355 --> 00:02:49.500
part in a million.

76
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.984
On k-means, eight of sixteen models passed

77
00:02:52.984 --> 00:02:54.470
and Opus scored best

78
00:02:54.538 --> 00:02:56.632
fifty-three point eight against

79
00:02:56.699 --> 00:02:58.320
forty-three point three.

80
00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.723
So: never store the big table

81
00:03:00.723 --> 00:03:02.366
keep a running minimum

82
00:03:02.366 --> 00:03:04.830
and let a bound make pruning exact.

83
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.260
As an exercise

84
00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.356
run the k-NN bound with k equal to twenty

85
00:03:09.356 --> 00:03:11.660
and count the rows it rescores.

86
00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:16.490
Next, DBSCAN and IVF-PQ.
